  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where does the water go when I flush?",
      "a": "It flows through a pipe from your home into a sewer under the street, then through bigger sewers, mostly downhill, to a treatment works where it is cleaned before returning to a river or the sea.",
      "asks": [
        "How do sewers work?",
        "Where does toilet waste go?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do sewers slope downhill?",
      "a": "Most sewers rely on gravity, not pumps, so they are laid at a steady slope. It must be steep enough to keep the flow moving and carry solids, but not so steep that the pipes end up very deep.",
      "asks": [
        "How do sewers flow without pumps?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a sewage pumping station do?",
      "a": "When a sewer would have to go too deep to keep sloping downhill, a pumping station lifts the sewage up into a higher pipe, so it can flow on by gravity.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do sewers need pumps?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a combined sewer overflow?",
      "a": "In older systems, rainwater and sewage share the same pipes. When heavy rain overfills them, overflow points release the excess, diluted sewage into rivers so it does not flood streets and homes.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does sewage go into rivers in storms?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who built London's sewers?",
      "a": "Joseph Bazalgette designed London's main sewer system after the Great Stink of 1858. Its large brick intercepting sewers carried waste away from the Thames in the city, and they are still in use today.",
      "asks": [
        "What was the Great Stink?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "After the Great Stink of 1858, Joseph Bazalgette built London's intercepting sewers: about 132 kilometres of main sewers fed by over 1,800 kilometres of street sewers.",
    "Most sewers flow by gravity, laid at a gentle, steady downhill slope."
  ],
